{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["An algorithm's specification is fixed and can be formally verified; a human-followed checklist is probabilistically executed and admits partial override.","Non-expert panels aggregate judgments, so their errors may be correlated or decorrelated differently from a single algorithmic run.","Experts have background knowledge that may allow them to reject a bad checklist item, while non-experts may lack the resources to do so."],"overturn_conditions":["If non-expert panels consistently identify and reject defective checklist items, the compliance-tracking hypothesis is weakened.","If expert intuition is shown to be more robust to distributional shift than checklist-guided non-experts, the reliability-gain claim is overturned.","If no evaluation condition can be constructed where the checklist and correct answer diverge, the distinction is empirically vacuous."],"shared_structure":["A structured procedure (checklist) replaces unstructured judgment (intuition) with explicitly verifiable steps.","Compliance with the procedure can causally produce correct outcomes without the agent understanding why.","Distinguishing genuine improvement from procedural mimicry requires an evaluation condition where the procedure's output and the correct answer diverge."],"source_memory_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"transfer_claims":["If an algorithm's output is correct only because its specification matches the evaluation distribution, this is analogous to checklist compliance tracking the right answer without genuine reliability.","The diagnostic distractor method transfers: plant an item that is irrelevant or wrong to separate rote compliance from calibrated use.","Reliability gains must be shown to hold across perturbations of the task distribution, not just on the training distribution, as with algorithm specification robustness."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by testing robustness to correlated checklist failures: if a checklist contains a planted defect that conflicts with an expert-known shortcut, non-expert panels following the checklist will systematically err (tracking compliance), while their overall non-defective performance must still exceed the expert's baseline, thus separating checklist-tracking from verifiable reliability.","counterconditions":["If non-expert panel accuracy on non-distractor cases does not exceed the expert's baseline, the reliability gain is not demonstrated.","If non-expert panels cannot be induced to follow a planted defective checklist item (they universally reject it), compliance tracking cannot be isolated from background knowledge.","If the expert's intuition is calibrated to match or exceed checklist performance on the task distribution, the panel provides no reliability advantage.","If the checklist does not admit any verifiable, decomposable steps corresponding to objectively checkable outcomes, the mechanism cannot produce the gain."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: reliability gains from non-expert panels require that the checklist actively replaces heuristic bias with verifiable steps, but mere compliance tracks correctness only when the checklist happens to encode the right procedure. To distinguish them, an evaluation must inject a diagnostic distractor—a checklist item that is causally irrelevant or subtly wrong for a subset of cases—so that genuine understanding of the checklist's rationale (or the panel's ability to flag and override defective items) produces different error patterns from rote compliance. If non-experts err on distractor cases where the checklist is wrong, their non-distractor accuracy must still exceed the expert's baseline to show reliability is not merely coincidental alignment with the checklist.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35}],"simulation_proposals":[]}